## Decision 02 - Byte-To-Char Pipeline

Problem: TMP/UI text churn allocates managed strings during subtitles and label refreshes.  
Solution: Route hash requests through UTF-8 slices, caller `Span<char>`, `CharBufferPool.BabelLease`, and `TMP_Text.SetCharArray`.  
Rejected Alternatives: `TMP_Text.text`, `SetText(string)`, `new string(span)`, and managed formatting.  
Scalability potential: Low strips rich tags; Middle keeps plain text; High/Ultra preserve rich text and spend saved CPU on visual-only TMP effects.  
Hardware Impact: Estimated 35-120 us and one managed string avoided per subtitle on i3/MX350-class CPU; profiler proof still pending.

## Decision 03 - CharBufferPool Native Arena

Problem: A managed-only char pool removed per-frame strings but did not satisfy the 64,000-char native arena requirement.  
Solution: Use 500 Babel slots x 128 chars in a `NativeArray<char>`; lease state uses `NativeBitArray`; TMP receives a preallocated `char[]` bridge because TMP has no `NativeArray<char>` overload.  
Rejected Alternatives: Unsafe TMP internals, runtime string bridge, dynamic char array growth.  
Scalability potential: Low/Middle/High/Ultra share the same arena; visual tier differences happen after text upload.  
Hardware Impact: 128 KB native arena, fixed 128-char copy to TMP bridge; expected bridge copy <3 us per subtitle.

## Decision 04 - H-Phi Vault Correction

Problem: The user mandate rejected private persistent native arrays where Vault ownership is available.  
Solution: Source Babel char arena, UTF-8 payload blob, and telemetry ring through `GlobalDataVault.GetBufferHandle<T>(..., SystemID.UI, ...)` when the Vault exists; keep local native fallback only before Vault boot.  
Rejected Alternatives: Moving `NativeParallelHashMap<uint,int2>` into Vault, because the current Vault API has no hash-map handle; adding new generic Vault container API in SHINOBU scope would be a compile-wall risk.  
Scalability potential: Low devices use one global arena allocation path; higher tiers can expand payload capacity through Vault sizing without changing subtitle code.  
Hardware Impact: Better allocation visibility and shared memory pressure accounting; no hot-path allocation.

## Decision 05 - Runtime DTO Alignment

Problem: Subtitle and localization commands cross systems and must not create ARM64 misaligned reads or CS1612 copy traps.  
Solution: Field-only sequential DTOs, 16-byte layout for `LocalizationEntryDTO`, `SubtitleCommandDTO`, `SubtitleStateDTO`, mock signals, and language-change signal.  
Rejected Alternatives: `[StructLayout(Pack=1)]`, auto-properties, class wrappers.  
Scalability potential: Same DTOs support NativeQueue, Burst jobs, and managed bridge rings.  
Hardware Impact: Avoids unaligned loads and defensive struct-copy mutation overhead.

